{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"1ab2f7ef-9aac-4a47-92a5-509fa54548e0","title":"Completing the square with an area model"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"1ab2f7ef-9aac-4a47-92a5-509fa54548e0","step":{"id":"s1","script":"To complete the square for x squared plus six x, start with a square of side x. Its area is x squared.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"area_model","region":"left","frame":{"x":[-0.8,8.8],"y":[-0.8,8.8],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"polyline","id":"base_square","points":[[0,0],[5,0],[5,5],[0,5]],"closed":true,"fill":true},{"kind":"text","at":[2.5,2.5],"text":"x²"},{"kind":"measure","from":[0,-0.4],"to":[5,-0.4],"label":"x"},{"kind":"measure","from":[-0.4,0],"to":[-0.4,5],"label":"x"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"start_expr","region":"C1","latex":"x^2+6x\\qquad(x>0)"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"1ab2f7ef-9aac-4a47-92a5-509fa54548e0","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Split six x into two equal rectangles, each with area three x. Place one along the top and one along the right.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"add_strips","on":"area_model","marks":[{"kind":"polyline","id":"top_strip","points":[[0,5],[5,5],[5,8],[0,8]],"closed":true,"fill":true},{"kind":"polyline","id":"right_strip","points":[[5,0],[8,0],[8,5],[5,5]],"closed":true,"fill":true},{"kind":"text","at":[2.5,6.5],"text":"3x"},{"kind":"text","at":[6.5,2.5],"text":"3x"},{"kind":"measure","from":[-0.4,5],"to":[-0.4,8],"label":"3"},{"kind":"measure","from":[5,-0.4],"to":[8,-0.4],"label":"3"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"split_expr","anchor":{"el":"start_expr","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"x^2+6x=x^2+3x+3x"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"1ab2f7ef-9aac-4a47-92a5-509fa54548e0","step":{"id":"s3","script":"The missing corner is three by three. Adding its area, nine, completes a square with side x plus three.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"complete_corner","on":"area_model","marks":[{"kind":"polyline","id":"corner","points":[[5,5],[8,5],[8,8],[5,8]],"closed":true,"fill":true},{"kind":"text","at":[6.5,6.5],"text":"9"},{"kind":"measure","from":[5,8.4],"to":[8,8.4],"label":"3"},{"kind":"measure","from":[8.4,5],"to":[8.4,8],"label":"3"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"square_identity","anchor":{"el":"split_expr","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"x^2+6x+9=(x+3)^2"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Why must we add 9 rather than 6 to complete this square?","expected_gist":"The missing corner has area 3 times 3, or 9."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"1ab2f7ef-9aac-4a47-92a5-509fa54548e0","step":{"id":"s4","script":"We added nine to make the square, so subtract nine to preserve the original expression. Half the x coefficient, then square it.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"whole_side","on":"area_model","marks":[{"kind":"measure","from":[0,8.75],"to":[8,8.75],"label":"x + 3"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"result","anchor":{"el":"square_identity","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\boxed{x^2+6x=(x+3)^2-9}"},{"op":"equation","id":"general_rule","anchor":{"el":"result","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"x^2+bx=\\left(x+\\frac b2\\right)^2-\\left(\\frac b2\\right)^2"}],"checkpoint":null}}
